"How many days until a warmer one?" asked for every day looks like a loop inside a loop. A **monotonic stack** answers it in one pass. Keep the indexes of days still waiting for an answer on a stack, their temperatures falling from bottom to top. A warmer day pops every cooler day on top and answers it:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	temps := []int{73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73}
	wait := make([]int, len(temps))
	stack := []int{}
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(wait)
}
```

```go
for i, t := range temps {
	for len(stack) > 0 && temps[stack[len(stack)-1]] < t {
		j := stack[len(stack)-1] // day j finally gets a warmer day
		stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
		wait[j] = i - j
	}
	stack = append(stack, i)
}
```

Store **indexes**, not temperatures: the index gives you both the distance `i - j` and the temperature `temps[j]`. Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so the loop inside the loop adds up to O(n).

Days still on the stack at the end never see a warmer day, and `make` already set their answer to 0. Popping while `<` leaves equal temperatures waiting, which is right, since an equal day isn't warmer.

