The biggest value in every window of k neighbors could take k steps per window. A **monotonic deque** keeps it at the front instead. Store indexes whose values fall from front to back:

- Before adding index `i`, pop from the back every index whose value is at most `nums[i]`. They can never be a window's maximum again, because `nums[i]` is at least as big and stays in the window longer.
- Push `i` on the back.
- If the front index has slid out of the window, pop it from the front.
- Once the first window is full, the front holds the maximum.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	nums := []int{1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7}
	k := 3
	window := []int{}
	maxes := []int{}
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(maxes)
}
```

```go
for i, x := range nums {
	for len(window) > 0 && nums[window[len(window)-1]] <= x {
		window = window[:len(window)-1] // pop the back
	}
	window = append(window, i)
	if window[0] <= i-k {
		window = window[1:] // the front slid out
	}
	if i >= k-1 {
		maxes = append(maxes, nums[window[0]])
	}
}
```

Items only ever join at the back, so a slice works as the deque: cut the end to pop the back, and reslice from 1 to pop the front. Every index enters and leaves once, so the whole scan is O(n).

